    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study investigates the interplay between pedagogical practices and teacher beliefs in Brazilian mathematics classrooms, examining how these factors relate to student achievement and learning disparities, with attention to race and gender. We conducted semi-structured interviews and classroom observations in four schools in São Paulo, Brazil. Sites were selected to represent different combinations of baseline average proficiency (p75 vs. p25) and intersectional inequalities average (p75 vs. p25) based on students' performance in the State's 2019 standardized math test. Findings show differences in pedagogical practices and teacher perceptions across groups, highlighting that more effective practices are prevalent in high-performing, low-gap environments. However, even in these settings, practices recognized as effective for equitable math teaching—such as using challenging tasks and addressing racial and gender issues—were often missing. This research contributes to mathematics pedagogical practices literature, offering implications for policy and teacher education.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fiorotto, S. & Santos, F. (2024). Exploring Pedagogical Practices for Equity and Excellence in Mathematics in Brazilian Classrooms. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/pk473fd7907. https://doi.org/10.25740/pk473fd7907.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
